{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact merge head 059fae34176834f8fa31d23e906577cd2a892720 on task/promote-layout-integration.\n\nHead verified clean and exact, with both declared parents present: 0a66e8595a4ca0b3378f7792d0eb5f94d3609db2 (current main) and 0426c3291d88c3d979fc1a9acc6ec3e1dca93d5c (the promotion candidate I approved earlier). Nothing on main was advanced.\n\nWHOLE-TREE COMPARISON RATHER THAN SPOT CHECKS, because the conditions warned that Git initially auto-merged stale versions. I enumerated every file under spike/ on current main - 63 of them - and compared each against its promoted counterpart. None is missing at the new layout. 35 are byte-identical after normalizing the module import prefix. 18 differ only in that prefix. The remaining files are accounted for individually: .gitignore is now root-appropriate, README.md is rewritten for a public audience, internal/nexus/session_privacy_test.go carries a one-line gofmt fix that clears the drift that has been reported on main for days, and the kernel trio is treated below.\n\nTHE KERNEL TRIO, which the conditions single out. checkpoint.go, kernel.go and kernel_test.go at this head are byte-identical to ff04227f442cac6eb3fd3e64507e5da8cb234918 - the key-rotation and checkpoint head I independently reviewed and approved earlier today - once the module path is normalized. All the rotation work is retained: rotationMarker, verifyCheckpointRotations, the rotation-spanning checkpoint fix and its tests are present and unaltered. The stale auto-merge the conditions warned about is not present in this head.\n\nA METHOD FAILURE OF MY OWN, recorded because it nearly became a false finding on kernel security code. My first comparison normalized only gitseq/spike/ to gitseq/, which is what the old layout required. This head also changes the module path to github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq, so every import differed and my comparison reported the kernel files as divergent from the approved head. Combined with the true observation that neither parent contains the rotation code, that reads exactly like unreviewed kernel code appearing in a merge, and I was one step from filing it as blocking. It was my normalizer. I re-ran the comparison against the full new module path and the files are identical. A reviewer who trusts a diff tool without checking what the tool was told to ignore will manufacture findings; recorded so the next reviewer of a path-changing merge does not repeat it.\n\nTHE LAYOUT BOUNDARY IS ENFORCED, not merely arranged. spike/ retains exactly nine files - the adversarial fixture commands gitseq-spike and gitseq-report, SPIKE-RESULTS.md, FORGE.md, compose.yaml and their support. layout_test.go pins the boundary in both directions: it requires go.mod, cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp, internal/kernel and the spike fixture paths to exist, requires spike/go.mod, spike/internal, spike/cmd/gs and spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp to be absent, and asserts the module declaration itself. Shipping code cannot drift back under the spike without failing a test.\n\nThe rest of the conditions: the late digest and statusview additions are at root and their tests pass there; the generated UI bytes match the sources at this head, which I verified by rebuilding uidist from scratch in a separate worktree and finding git status clean; the symlink repair from 0426c329 remains present as EvalSymlinks in spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main_test.go.\n\nGates at this head: go vet ./... clean; go test ./... green across all fifteen packages under the new module path, including both spike fixture packages and the root layout guard; go test -race ./... green; go build ./... ok; git diff --check clean; checkout clean at the exact head afterwards.\n\nTWO OBSERVATIONS, neither blocking.\n\nFirst, the public module path is now github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq, pinned by layout_test.go and threaded through every import in the tree. That is the decision that fixes the public repository name. It is correct to make it here rather than at push time, but it is cheap to change now and expensive later, so it deserves an explicit confirmation from whoever owns the account before this merges rather than being inherited silently from a promotion review.\n\nSecond, the rotation work's approval is not discoverable from the merge graph. ff04227f is not an ancestor of this head, and neither declared parent contains the rotation code; it arrived through conflict resolution during the integration. The code is approved and I have verified it byte-for-byte, so nothing is wrong with the content. But a future reader running git merge-base --is-ancestor ff04227f 059fae34 gets a negative answer and would reasonably conclude the rotation work is absent. It also means merging this head lands the rotation change into main by way of a layout promotion. The conditions disclose this, so it is not a finding, but the merge artifact should say plainly that this head carries ff04227f's content, so the approval remains findable after the branches are gone.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:532625ad22dc3fb0ea8a214874b70343570eb187","head":"059fae34176834f8fa31d23e906577cd2a892720","verdict":"approved"}}